From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 06:06:18 -0400
Subject: fix: the defect round of 2026-10-18

The BACKLOG's defects, each with a test that goes through the user's path:

- A tab pinned while selected comes back selected after a restart: the
  session writes the selected tab after a move, not only after a change of
  selected tab, an insert or a close (S-212, pins H18).
- Spaces (S-145): unpinning an essential files it in the Space on screen,
  and an essential is no Space's pin; a switch from an essential lands on
  the Space's own tab; going to a tab shows no other tab of its Space on
  the way, nor wakes one; an activation from outside the sidebar brings the
  tab's Space on screen; a tab moved or dragged into a new window opens it
  on the Space it came from; a restored Space with no name keeps its tabs;
  the switcher's chips stay the same views while the sidebar's width
  animates. An essential matches every "@Space" in the command bar.
- The Space left slides out as the new one comes in (S-146).
- A click on the open sidebar's toggle, the pointer left on it, no longer
  lets the sidebar slide out again two seconds later (S-219).
- Pins (S-142): another Space's hidden tab no longer puts the pins after it
  under the Clear line, and a tab opened from a pin or an essential leaves
  the pinned run; Cmd-W and a row's close button share one rule, and outside
  a normal window with Spaces Cmd-W closes a pinned tab; a home taken before
  the pin's first load is settled once that load has finished; the
  bookmarks conversion sets its preference only when it made something.
- The Clear line shows whenever there are rows under it, pins or none, a
  private window's included (S-138).
- Move to Space takes a tab out of its folder, to just after the outermost
  one, and a new tab that lands in a folder of another Space's tabs leaves
  it, so a folder never spans two Spaces (S-139, folders F37).
- Folders (S-140): Move Up and Move Down pass rows the user cannot see; the
  header toggles on the left button alone and is a named, focusable button;
  the drop highlight takes the sidebar's colours.
- A sleeping row's favicon dims itself in its own paint, and a hover no
  longer rebuilds the folder's glyph (S-143).
- The number badges hide when the window stops being active, Cmd-1 to 9 use
  the badges' own list, and the badges follow the Animate setting (S-144).
- The tab group header's title and the Clear line's word draw no subpixel
  text on a layer that is not opaque (S-214).
- stedding_browser_tests depends on the browser's packed resources, so a
  build of the test targets alone serves the current welcome page (S-213).
- Skipping the welcome flow's engine step keeps DuckDuckGo as the choice,
  and Chromium's own chooser no longer opens; a second pick no longer
  reaches Chromium's choice check (S-134).
- On the Mac, splitting the tab with a new one is Control-Cmd-N, which Arc
  leaves alone; Chromium's Option-Cmd-N still splits while Arc is not
  running (S-79).

Why: each item is a defect a user meets, behaviour its spec states, or a
crash a non-official build would take.
Removable when: never as a whole; the next rebase folds each fix into the patch
that owns its code (patches/README.md, the round-patch exception).
